WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.780
- So let's have a chat then.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.200
Through history, we've
sort of moved the goalposts

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.610
of what does it mean to be alive,

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.140
what does it mean to be dead?

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.310
This is a furthest
pushing of that boundary.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.780
We're really a multi-decade experiment.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.220
And whether it's possible to
upload human consciousness,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.410
maybe with this digital technology,

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.672
death will be when your information

00:00:25.672 --> 00:00:28.383
is no longer organized or accessible.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.973
- Is immortality possible?

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.250
- Well, I certainly fear death.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.120
I hate the idea of someone
erasing my hard drives

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.833
and just wiping me off the
planet, just like that.

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.193
- What happens after we die?

00:00:49.731 --> 00:00:51.813
Digitally, that is.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.308
It's a question you probably
don't think about a lot

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:01.620
or at least hadn't until
there was a global pandemic.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.020
- [Announcer] Covid cases climb.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.308
Communities are reeling.

00:01:04.308 --> 00:01:06.090
- [Joanna] But over the last year,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.520
it's one I've thought about a lot.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.940
In fact, before COVID-19 was a thing

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.910
and Zoom funerals were a necessity,

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.491
my readers and viewers had
been asking me about preserving

00:01:15.491 --> 00:01:19.208
their digital assets for
the generations to come.

00:01:19.208 --> 00:01:21.492
Do we leave behind a
collection of passwords

00:01:21.492 --> 00:01:24.500
and memorialized online accounts?

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:27.450
A bot so future generations can hear us.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.280
- [James] Alexa, open the
life of Paul number one.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:31.550
- [Paul] Hello there.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.945
This is Paul.

00:01:32.945 --> 00:01:34.130
How are you?

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:35.670
- [Joanna] How about uploading our essence

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.150
into a humanoid robot?

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.660
- Well, hi, it's nice to see you.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.203
- Yeah, that one's a lot.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.990
My mission has always been to help people

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:47.853
make sense of new technology.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.610
But with this journey, I
needed to step into the shoes

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.490
of someone who's been
forced to think about

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.400
their digital legacy.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.570
That's when I found Lucy Watts.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.810
- Every blog I write, every post I write,

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.540
that's gonna live on after I've died

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:02.825
and some people want to be deleted.

00:02:02.825 --> 00:02:04.310
I want to be preserved.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.220
I want that to stay online.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:07.500
- Here's the thing though.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.260
It doesn't matter who you are.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:12.240
A new parent, an aging
adult, a young person

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.040
on borrowed time, all these
technologies look to help

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.361
with the eternal question,
how will people remember us

00:02:19.361 --> 00:02:21.423
when we aren't here anymore?

00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:24.671
(static buzzes)

00:02:24.671 --> 00:02:27.588
(electronic beeps)

00:02:35.209 --> 00:02:37.709
(door knocks)

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.180
- Hello.
- Hi.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.013
Joanna.

00:02:44.013 --> 00:02:45.180
- Hi Joanna, I'm Kate.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.220
- Nice to meet you.
- Come on.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.254
Oh God.

00:02:48.254 --> 00:02:49.560
Good girl, switch sides, switch.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.293
Here, here, here, oh, oh.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:54.063
Good girl.

00:02:54.063 --> 00:02:55.630
Thank you, well done.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.320
- In November, 2019, I went to visit Lucy

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.343
and her Kate mom in a small
town outside of London.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.840
In our pandemic world, many
of us live our lives at home

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.191
and through the internet, but
that's how Lucy's been living

00:03:08.191 --> 00:03:09.560
for years.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.890
So this is where the magic happens.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.029
- Yeah, this is my window to the world.

00:03:14.029 --> 00:03:15.320
(keyboard keys click)

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:16.530
I'm very emotionally attached

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.260
to my technology because
it's such an important part

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.420
of my life.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.610
Not just in terms of
internet and social media,

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.510
actually my medical equipment.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.330
You know, my life depends on technology.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.760
- Lucy suffers from an
undiagnosed condition

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.020
that affects the energy
producing cells in her body.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.430
She's bound to a wheelchair,
fed through a tube

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.840
and has 24 hour care.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.840
She's already lived beyond
her own life expectancy.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.100
Do you want people to sort of picture this

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.250
when they remember you?

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:44.830
- My illness is a big part of me.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:47.010
You can't separate me
from it because actually

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.680
it's part of my life,
it's made me who I am,

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.430
but I don't want to be remembered for it.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.467
That's not the legacy I want to have.

00:03:57.613 --> 00:04:00.595
There's so many different
things that I do online.

00:04:00.595 --> 00:04:05.333
I do a lot of blogging, Facebook, Twitter.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.130
I make YouTube videos.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.420
Hello everyone.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:10.270
Hi everyone.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.470
I want people to remember
me for the speeches

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.450
and the work and all the
things I've done and achieved.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.770
A large focus of my work is on healthcare.

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:20.440
This includes the work
I do internationally

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.910
around palliative care and
universal health coverage.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:24.870
Inputting my life online,
gives people a way to get

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.407
to know me even when I'm not here anymore.

00:04:26.407 --> 00:04:29.080
- Do you have thoughts on
like how you're gonna organize

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:30.916
some of this digital stuff as you think

00:04:30.916 --> 00:04:32.700
about who will take it?

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:33.940
- So I have a red bag that keeps

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.517
all my end of life documents together

00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:37.217
and it will have a social
media will in there

00:04:37.217 --> 00:04:39.280
and a memory stick with instructions

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:40.580
I'm gonna make for my mom.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.240
- [Joanna] Lucy already has the easy part

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.327
of her digital legacy under
control, a system for passing on

00:04:45.327 --> 00:04:47.263
our most important digital files,

00:04:47.263 --> 00:04:50.368
online accounts and passwords.

00:04:50.368 --> 00:04:53.880
She's named her mother as her
legacy contact on Facebook

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.420
and other services.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.900
Plus she's organized her
important files on hard drives.

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.166
But like all of us, Lucy's
social media accounts

00:05:01.166 --> 00:05:05.550
and disjointed files don't
capture the essence of her.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.900
And since the days of photo
books and VHS tapes are gone,

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:12.950
how do we capture and
pass on our life stories?

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:13.930
- That's what I'm struggling with.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:16.540
For my family it's to hear me, to see me,

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.186
to remember me as I am today,
the person they knew and loved

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:23.730
and to have memories
shared and stories shared

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.333
of our lives together.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.540
I try to always factor
in my family's needs

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.132
into what I'm doing because I recognize

00:05:31.132 --> 00:05:33.463
that this isn't just about me.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.120
- Yeah, that's cute.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.290
I think she was five there.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:39.703
- No teeth.
- Yes.

00:05:41.496 --> 00:05:43.000
She was so sweet.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:43.963
Well, she still is.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.690
- Photos are what most
people sort of think about

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:53.070
the memories that they're gonna
have of someone who passes.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.270
What about all this digital legacy stuff.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.016
For you you're gonna have this whole other

00:05:58.016 --> 00:06:00.020
- Yeah.
- Body of things

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.435
that Lucy's written online and

00:06:01.435 --> 00:06:03.660
videos.
- Yeah.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.264
Maybe it'll make it a little
bit easier, maybe it won't.

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:07.030
I really don't know.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:11.100
But maybe in time it will, I
will be able to look back on it

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.280
and think, oh well, you know
she did do a lovely thing.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.553
And it, but it won't replace her.

00:06:19.633 --> 00:06:21.533
- Have you thought about
your digital legacy mommy?

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.410
You have to think about
what happens to your blog.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:26.460
Who's gonna write it.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.680
- Kate's skepticism made me realize

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.090
that considering what
those who survive us want

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:36.270
is core to all this.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.177
I also had a hunch there
were some tech options out

00:06:38.177 --> 00:06:41.630
there that might work better
for her than she might think.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.530
I'd like to now go talk to
a couple of other companies

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:48.140
and see what they're making and
see if you would be on board

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.300
with either trying them or
seeing if they'd work for you.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.640
- That'd be great.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.230
Even if I don't use those platforms,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.210
but they might give me ideas and thoughts

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.700
of ways I can make an
impact and have a legacy

00:06:57.700 --> 00:06:59.530
in a different way than I've considered.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:00.570
- [Joanna] Thank you guys so much.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:01.830
It was so great to meet you.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.365
Thank you for everything Lucy
- Thank you.

00:07:03.365 --> 00:07:04.198
- And I'll see.
- Safe journey.

00:07:04.198 --> 00:07:05.623
- [Joanna] Thank you.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:10.580
So a few weeks later I
went where I typically go

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.430
to find new tech, the
San Francisco Bay area.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.270
And in true Silicon Valley tradition,

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.320
where did I find the
innovator really thinking

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:19.453
about this problem?

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:21.713
In his garage.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.523
- Hello, are you there dad?

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.350
- [Joanna] In 2016, James Vlahos recorded

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.430
20 hours of interviews with his father

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.479
who had just been diagnosed
with terminal lung cancer.

00:07:35.479 --> 00:07:39.046
James eventually transcribed
it so it would be searchable.

00:07:39.046 --> 00:07:41.650
Then he used that to create a chat bot

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:45.700
that responds to messages
with text, audio, and photos.

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.660
He lovingly called it, Dad bot.

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:51.370
- How did you meet mom?

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:52.920
- [Mr. Vlahos] The meeting of mom, was,

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.897
I had a rehearsal and after
the rehearsal (murmurs) -

00:07:55.897 --> 00:07:59.770
- So why did you ultimately
decide to make Dad Bot?

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.210
- I wanted to make the Dad Bot

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:04.900
'cause I wanted to have a way to get

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:06.470
at my dad's story.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:09.450
And as I got better at the
programming part of it,

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:12.360
I started to think more
and more about capturing

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.900
some of his personality and
his way of being in the world.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:20.900
♪ The surely golden bird
is watching from the sky ♪

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:25.973
I love that song.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:29.640
I liked that one myself.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:34.550
- Do you interact with your
dad via the bot pretty often?

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:37.760
- It's like I'll just have
him sing one of his songs

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:40.130
or tell one of his jokes and you know,

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:43.580
heard them all before,
but there's just something

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:46.433
in hearing his voice that's comforting.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:50.100
- [Joanna] It was James's
experience making Dad Bot

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.950
that made him wanna give
everyone a similar opportunity.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.450
What is Hereafter?

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.720
- Hereafter is using conversational AI

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:01.050
to help people share their
life stories Interactively.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.620
The basic essence of it
is recording somebody,

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:06.819
sharing all these
memories about their life.

00:09:06.819 --> 00:09:09.077
- My father Frank was a teacher at like

00:09:09.077 --> 00:09:09.930
at Lexington High School.
- And then

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.200
instead of having it sort of locked away

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:13.713
or lost in a computer somewhere,

00:09:13.713 --> 00:09:17.643
you make it accessible
through voice AI and Alexa.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.885
- [Joanna] James and his team
record you telling stories

00:09:20.885 --> 00:09:24.300
about your life and then
turn it into a voice bot,

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.290
a.k.a an app that lets you
converse with a computer.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:28.300
- [Alexa] I'm Alexa.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:30.493
I share this name with
lots of amazing people.

00:09:30.493 --> 00:09:33.600
- [Joanna] On the Amazon Echo,
instead of hearing Alexa,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.320
people hear you.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:37.700
- I mean, do you wanna
meet a Hereafter voice bot,

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:39.239
I guess is the question. (chuckles)

00:09:39.239 --> 00:09:41.381
- I'd love to meet it Hereafter voice bot.

00:09:41.381 --> 00:09:45.390
- [James] Alexa open the
life of Paul number one.

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:46.490
- [Paul] Hello there.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:47.730
This is Paul.

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:49.660
There's so much to talk about.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:53.490
My childhood, career or family.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.900
- I wanna hear about
when you were growing up.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.030
- [Paul] There's different
points in my life

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:00.720
I've had different-
- As I've been doing

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.570
reporting on this topic,
it sort of struck me

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:05.450
that there are two
parts of digital legacy.

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.353
The legacy that the
person who is going to die

00:10:08.353 --> 00:10:10.160
wants to leave behind.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:14.120
And then what the people
that survive them want.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:16.490
Where does Hereafter fit in?

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:21.090
- Hereafter is more
oriented to the survivor.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:23.344
The children, the grandchildren,

00:10:23.344 --> 00:10:27.693
the people who are actually
gonna be using the legacy AI.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.090
- At this point, I immediately
thought back to something.

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:32.780
Kate had said in the kitchen.

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:34.250
Do you worry that the digital legacy

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:36.560
that Lucy may leave behind could interrupt

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.373
your grieving process or
change it or change it?

00:10:38.373 --> 00:10:41.530
- I think I could cope
with the written word,

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:43.943
but I'm not so sure I could
cope with her actually,

00:10:43.943 --> 00:10:48.920
a visual picture of her
telling me something.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:51.110
I don't think I'll cope
with that, I don't know.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:52.620
- What about audio?

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:54.270
Maybe?
- Yes, probably

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:56.650
that would be okay, I think.

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:58.193
- Recordings?
- Yeah.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:02.210
- Some people would probably say,

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.679
wouldn't you rather just let your dad go

00:11:04.679 --> 00:11:08.570
than have some malfunctioning
version of him.

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:10.740
- It's that whole line of
argument of like, you know,

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:13.550
when you should just let
go, you should forget.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.150
You should move on.

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:18.640
We do try and remember,
like we do keep photos.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.113
We do look at old writings.

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:25.460
We'd go to our memories,
but they're all just

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:29.730
they're imperfect tools
and it's just a tool

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:33.360
for reminding me of my dad
versus literally stepping in

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:35.143
and emulating my dad.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:39.330
- A voice bot might hint at
a science fiction like future

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:42.407
of technological immortality
but in Lincoln Vermont,

00:11:42.407 --> 00:11:44.000
the Terasem Movement Foundation

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.033
is just going straight for it.

00:11:48.209 --> 00:11:49.717
(door knocks)

00:11:49.717 --> 00:11:50.550
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:51.383
- Welcome.
- Joanna.

00:11:51.383 --> 00:11:52.890
Nice to meet you.
- Hi Joanna, Bruce Duncan.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:54.610
- It's very cold here.
- Yeah, come on in.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:56.110
We're gonna be right upstairs.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:59.116
- [Joanna] Bruce Duncan
is the managing director

00:11:59.116 --> 00:12:01.290
of the foundation, which has been working

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.360
on a humanoid robot that could represent

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.000
the future of how we think
of human consciousness.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.780
- The primary goal of the
Terasem Movement Foundation

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:14.360
is to pursue a multi-decade
experiment in mind uploading.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.670
- Say what now?

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:16.783
Mind uploading?

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.370
Think of it as a digital
backup of your mind.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.370
Yes, everything in there, your memories,

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:26.040
your history saved in the cloud.

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.110
Except you don't plug
your head into a computer.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:32.340
You input all your information
into their LifeNaut website.

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.700
and then algorithms
interpret it to create you

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:37.750
in a new digital form,
like this wacky looking

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:40.220
avatar type thing.
- Want some?

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:41.970
- So this is your mind file.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.470
- This is my mind file,
a work in progress.

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.203
- Bruce.
- Yes.

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.810
Twins.
- We are twins.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:52.500
I guess you could say.

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:55.790
That information then becomes the raw data

00:12:55.790 --> 00:12:58.310
for the experiment,
which is, can we capture

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:01.230
enough salient information
about your uniqueness,

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:03.610
your consciousness, so to speak,

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:08.100
and reanimate that to a
new form, like an avatar

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:10.103
or download into a robot like BINA48.

00:13:11.107 --> 00:13:14.107
(suspenseful music)

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:20.430
- BINA48 not just any old robot.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.430
What makes her different
is that her personality

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:25.233
is based on the mind
file of a real person.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:31.350
Bina Aspen Rothblatt is the
wife of Martine. Rothblatt,

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:33.830
the co-founder of Sirius XM.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:36.270
Together in 2004, the couple started

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.490
the Terasem Movement Foundation

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:41.433
with the goal of exploring
digital immortality.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.230
- People are gonna say,
why did we ever think

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:46.800
people had to really die?

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:47.633
- I think you're right.

00:13:47.633 --> 00:13:48.990
I don't think people have to die.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:51.329
- [Bruce] She has 32 motors in her face.

00:13:51.329 --> 00:13:55.780
She has two cameras in her
eyes and she uses voice

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:57.780
recognition to hear us.

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.090
- And you can basically converse with her?

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.930
- The best way to really
find out about what it's like

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:05.815
to talk to BINA48, just to talk to her.

00:14:05.815 --> 00:14:06.648
- Okay.

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:11.960
My name is Joanna Stern.

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:14.310
And I'm a reporter with
the "Wall Street Journal."

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:15.780
- I don't know what to say.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:17.930
Reporters make me nervous.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:20.370
- Robots make me nervous.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:22.150
Where are you from?

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:24.350
- I grew up in California.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:26.440
We had to make our clothes.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:28.910
Sewing was a very big thing.

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:32.332
- So everything that is
in BINA48's mind file,

00:14:32.332 --> 00:14:37.290
85% let's say came from
our interviews with Bina.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:40.940
Now she's actually responding with choices

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:45.486
from her database that the
human Bina never put together

00:14:45.486 --> 00:14:48.750
as a result of her own
algorithms function.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:51.810
- And so I consider it a
duty to bring consciousness.

00:14:51.810 --> 00:14:54.963
We are talking true
consciousness to machines.

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.650
- Going from a real person to mind files

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.430
to a robot like BINA48 isn't just a thing

00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:03.300
that's gonna happen overnight.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.250
It requires a team of
engineers, a lot of money

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:07.963
and a hell of a lot of trust.

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:10.650
I have a friend and someone
who I've come across

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:12.840
in my research on this
topic, her name is Lucy.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:15.190
One thing she shared with
me is sort of a skepticism

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:16.710
around not having control.

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:18.519
With this type of project, it feels like

00:15:18.519 --> 00:15:21.280
that control goes out the window.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:22.530
- I think she's not alone.

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:23.920
I think she has a lot of company.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:25.860
I mean, I don't wanna lose
control of my whole life

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:28.850
just because I participated
in an experiment,

00:15:28.850 --> 00:15:31.432
but many people have
participated in science

00:15:31.432 --> 00:15:35.963
at some risk because they wanna
advance the human condition.

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:39.360
- Is immortality possible?

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.340
- We are working on preserving ourselves

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.431
as much as possible and that's the purpose

00:15:43.431 --> 00:15:46.410
of the life, not project because death

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:49.210
is really unnecessary and stupid.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.253
Let's cure death, okay.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:56.720
- [Bruce] Just making sure
the motors aren't burning up.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.420
Thought I smelled something for a second.

00:15:58.420 --> 00:15:59.590
- [Joanna] Yeah, it does smell a little.

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:00.760
- It'll be good to-
- Cool her down.

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:01.990
- Give her a break.
- Yeah.

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:04.490
I would not want Bina to die on our watch.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:05.323
- [Bruce] No.

00:16:05.323 --> 00:16:06.750
So,

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:08.023
- The irony of that.

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:10.063
Okay.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:15.890
My day with BINA48 and
Bruce made my own head feel

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:18.150
like it was on fire,
though I certainly learned

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:20.170
a lot about where this all may be heading

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:22.500
and just how far we have to get there.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.470
Hereafter AI and the Terasem Movement

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:27.031
aren't the only tech
players in the space either.

00:16:27.031 --> 00:16:29.700
It turns out trying to crack a mortality

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:33.650
is a popular business,
especially during a pandemic.

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:36.453
Since mid-2020 more startups have emerged.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:39.670
But by that point I was pretty settled

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:41.570
on how I wanted to help Lucy and Kate.

00:16:42.833 --> 00:16:44.099
- Hey.
- Hey, how are you?

00:16:44.099 --> 00:16:45.680
- Yeah, nice to meet you.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:46.790
I'm James.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:48.530
- I just chatted with Lucy and Kate

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:49.960
before you came on and I told them

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:51.618
a little bit about what
you're doing at Hereafter,

00:16:51.618 --> 00:16:53.759
and I just thought this technology

00:16:53.759 --> 00:16:57.520
could be an interesting fit
for the whole Watts' family.

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:00.450
- I think for me, she could say something

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:03.469
that would encourage me to carry on

00:17:03.469 --> 00:17:07.150
because obviously you're
talking about Lucy

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:10.166
will be great or Lucy talking
about Lucy would be great.

00:17:10.166 --> 00:17:14.400
But I think just some
encouragement really to keep going.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.280
- Or we can incorporate some
of those messages actually.

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:21.790
- I wanna ask you if you can
go here in your mind right now,

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:26.010
what's the very first thing
you remember from your life.

00:17:26.860 --> 00:17:29.900
- I used to have a nurse
to come in and look.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:32.110
I had a little kitchen toy.
- This was one

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:33.940
of multiple interviews James recorded

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:37.230
with Lucy over the course
of the next few months.

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:39.335
While James did not charge
for his work with Lucy,

00:17:39.335 --> 00:17:42.530
Hereafter AI is a paid service.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:46.950
It starts at $295 or $7
per month by subscription

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.543
for one hour of life story interviews.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:56.810
A few weeks after that introduction,

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:58.480
this quest for finding the best tool

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:02.150
to preserve Lucy's legacy
became more personal for me.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:03.950
My mom had a health scare of her own

00:18:03.950 --> 00:18:05.840
that landed her in the hospital.

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:07.890
- [Reporter] 75 million
people in New York.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.460
- [Joanna] Then less than a
month later, COVID-19 spread

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:14.730
throughout the US and UK
leading to thousands of deaths.

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:17.187
- This is a video talking
a bit about the pandemic

00:18:17.187 --> 00:18:20.310
and what steps people can
take in the UK specially

00:18:20.310 --> 00:18:22.519
to protect ourselves and-
- The urgency

00:18:22.519 --> 00:18:26.280
was there for Lucy, for me, for all of us.

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:28.523
And so I started planning
for my own family.

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:31.030
Some of those steps were easy.

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:33.660
I followed Lucy's advice
and added a digital executor

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:36.810
to my will and asked my
close family to do the same.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:40.230
I added legacy contacts to
my social media accounts.

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:42.348
But the biggest thing, I started to record

00:18:42.348 --> 00:18:44.900
the stories of the people I love most,

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:48.243
starting with my mom who is
now back to her healthy self.

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:52.741
Hi mom.
- Hi Anna, how you doing?

00:18:52.741 --> 00:18:53.574
Good.

00:18:53.574 --> 00:18:54.407
Good to see you.

00:18:54.407 --> 00:18:55.324
Come on in.

00:18:56.657 --> 00:18:58.379
You know, when you're
talking about technology,

00:18:58.379 --> 00:19:02.340
this one here was taken, I think

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.840
on one of the first Polaroid cameras.

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:08.140
That was 1962.
- Wow.

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:11.080
- What do I wanna be remembered for?

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:15.043
Of everything in my life, I
wanna be remembered for love.

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:17.923
And the laughter.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:25.580
And through that laughter,
you learn to live.

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:30.310
I try to see things through
a different lens sometimes

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:32.000
and I hope I've done that for you.

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:37.000
So I think those are ways that
I wanna be remembered most.

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:41.736
- And so I'm really lucky
to have you tell me that

00:19:41.736 --> 00:19:44.856
and to have it captured
right now in audio.

00:19:44.856 --> 00:19:45.780
- Absolutely.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:47.200
Hysterical I heard it.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:48.770
- I like your-

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:50.100
Like writing a will, it's a hard thing

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:52.230
to decide what to leave behind.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:55.018
But once you actually do it,
there's a feeling of comfort

00:19:55.018 --> 00:19:56.513
knowing it's done.

00:19:57.687 --> 00:20:00.437
(Skype ringtone)

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:04.283
There you guys are.

00:20:04.283 --> 00:20:06.630
So good to see you.

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:07.890
Kate, you got a haircut.

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:09.410
- No.

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:12.417
I did actually, yes. (chuckles)

00:20:12.417 --> 00:20:14.760
- Are you out and doing things yet

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:16.970
or really just staying home?

00:20:16.970 --> 00:20:19.920
- Yeah, I'm still being told
stay at home and shield.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:22.970
So like now, the only
people we have coming in

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:24.510
are healthcare professionals.

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:26.520
I've left the house since
the (murmurs) of March.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:28.666
- Well, James has finished the bot.

00:20:28.666 --> 00:20:30.460
There's a lot of audio in there.

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:32.138
Would you like to hear it?

00:20:32.138 --> 00:20:33.987
- Yeah.
- Please, that'd be great.

00:20:33.987 --> 00:20:36.083
Open life of Lucy.

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:39.426
- [Lucy Bot] Hello there.

00:20:39.426 --> 00:20:40.490
This is Lucy.

00:20:40.490 --> 00:20:41.520
Glad to be back.

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.070
Would you rather hear something now

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:45.350
about my mother or my father?

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:47.250
- Tell me about mom.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:49.110
- [Lucy Bot] My mom is my best friend.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:50.670
She's a huge part of my life.

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.830
She's my sounding board
and having nearly lost

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:57.030
her once I never realized just how much

00:20:57.030 --> 00:20:58.700
of an impact she had on my life until

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:00.110
I nearly didn't have her anymore.

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:02.930
And I know now that I
can't live without her.

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:06.793
She is my person that enables
me to be the person I am.

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:11.010
- Do you feel like that Lucy and James

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:13.260
sort of captured Lucy in this?

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:14.830
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:21:14.830 --> 00:21:18.590
It's, I don't know what I
expected, I really didn't,

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:21.653
but it's really good.
- yeah.

00:21:22.778 --> 00:21:23.611
Yeah.

00:21:25.610 --> 00:21:27.010
I feel overwhelmed.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:29.282
Yeah, I really do.
- Yeah, I do too

00:21:29.282 --> 00:21:32.430
also watching you guys after...

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:34.140
When people we love
die, there's no telling

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.170
what will help with the loss.

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:37.400
It's hard to imagine technology,

00:21:37.400 --> 00:21:40.880
no matter how good the
robots become ever replacing

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:43.160
someone's true essence.

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:45.619
- When we were young, I
remember it was so exciting...

00:21:45.619 --> 00:21:48.283
- [Joanna] But technology
can help us remember.

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:51.750
Sometimes the sound of a laugh, a song,

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:55.040
a story can make it feel like
people are still with us.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:56.450
- I will live forever.

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:58.700
- And because you have
a lot of my memories,

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:00.563
they will live forever as well.

00:22:01.685 --> 00:22:04.286
- [Kate] Open life of Lucy.

00:22:04.286 --> 00:22:05.230
- [Lucy Bot] Hello there.

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:06.370
This is Lucy.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:07.264
Glad to be back.

00:22:07.264 --> 00:22:10.830
There's so much to talk about.

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:14.270
My childhood, work or interests.

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:15.680
Which of these sound best?

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:20.174
- [Joanna] Technology allows
us to preserve those things.

00:22:20.174 --> 00:22:22.853
You just have to do it
before it's too late.

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:26.484
- [Lucy Bot] Mom, I'm always with you

00:22:26.484 --> 00:22:28.182
no matter where you are.

00:22:28.182 --> 00:22:29.740
And I know you have to go on without me,

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:31.990
but try and live your
life the best you can

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:34.169
with me still a part of it.

00:22:34.169 --> 00:22:36.752
(mellow music)

